<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zeyu Gan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hao Yi
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yong Liu
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Gaoling School of Artificial Intelligence
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Renmin University of China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{zygan,yihao,liuyonggsai}@ruc.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) has become a pivotal approach for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, a significant theoretical gap persists, as traditional token-level RL frameworks fail to align with the reasoning-level nature of complex, multi-step thought processes like Chain-of-Thought (CoT). To address this challenge, we introduce CoT-Space, a novel theoretical framework that recasts LLM reasoning from a discrete token-prediction task to an optimization process within a continuous, reasoning-level semantic space. By analyzing this process from both a noise perspective and a risk perspective, we demonstrate that the convergence to an optimal CoT length is a natural consequence of the fundamental trade-off between underfitting and overfitting. Furthermore, extensive experiments provide strong empirical validation for our theoretical findings. Our framework not only provides a coherent explanation for empirical phenomena such as overthinking but also offers a solid theoretical foundation to guide the future development of more effective and generalizable reasoning agents.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning is a critical capability for Large Language Models (LLMs), and there has been a significant focus on developing methods to improve it. One of the most well-known frameworks is Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Under the CoT framework, a promising approach to enhance LLM reasoning is “test-time scaling”, also known as “slow thinking” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This branch of methods generally falls into two categories: external and internal.
External methods extend the LLM’s output by introducing additional steps, often through a tree-structured search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. These techniques, which do not require extra training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, include popular frameworks such as Monte-Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and self-consistency (also known as Best-of-N) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
In contrast, internal methods integrate the slow-thinking capability directly into the model itself through post-training. This is achieved either by supervised fine-tuning (SFT), a process often referred to as distillation, or by reinforcement learning (RL).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in reasoning models such as DeepSeek’s R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, OpenAI’s o1 and o3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and Qwen’s QwQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> have intensified the focus on the internal slow thinking methods, and especially using RL to enhance LLM reasoning. This training paradigm is often termed <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero</span>, indicating that the model is post-trained directly from its pre-training checkpoint using RL, bypassing the SFT stage.
The results of <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero</span>-like approach are encouraging, as they demonstrate the potential for LLMs to develop reasoning capabilities through self-exploration with minimal supervision. This observation aligns with the core philosophy of RL: by setting a high-level, global objective, the policy can be guided to discover an optimal solution independently. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the application of RL to LLM reasoning shares similarities with classical RL scenarios. For instance, as shown in subgraph (a), in a classic RL task like Bricks Breaker<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Bricks Breaker is a video game, the goal is to destroy the bricks by shooting a ball at them.</span></span></span>, the agent’s goal is to break more bricks. Without explicit instructions on specific strategies, the agent may discover a highly effective method, such as hitting the ball behind the top row of bricks to achieve a higher score. This kind of emergent behavior is a common outcome in classical RL.
Similarly, in the context of LLM reasoning, as shown in subgraph (b), the policy model is given the broad goal of providing a correct answer. As RL training progresses, the model converges on an effective CoT pattern, demonstrating its ability to learn complex reasoning pathways from a simple, high-level reward signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the discovery that models can automatically converge on a suitable CoT, various RL-based methods have been proposed for more effective <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero</span>-like post-training, including GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, VC-PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and VAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
However, despite these practical advances, a significant gap remains in the theoretical understanding of LLM internal “slow thinking” via RL. Firstly, the surprising “overthinking” phenomenon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> suggests there is an optimal CoT length for the reasoning process. Although empirical observations show different convergence patterns with various RL recipes, the precise mechanism driving this behavior remains unknown. Secondly, the generalization ability of LLM reasoning tasks is not yet well understood; while we observe this phenomenon, the underlying principles are not fully analyzed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A central challenge to the theoretical analysis of LLM reasoning via RL is the misalignment between the traditional RL theoretical framework and the CoT paradigm. The conventional framework for RL with LLMs operates at the token level, where each action is the selection and concatenation of a single token to the current output. In contrast, the CoT approach functions as a higher-level reasoning framework, where the output is generated step-by-step, with each thought containing multiple tokens to solve a specific reasoning step.
To address this limitation, we propose CoT-Space, a novel reasoning-level theoretical framework for LLM reasoning via RL. This framework is designed to enable a more precise theoretical analysis of this domain. We further validate its effectiveness by performing two corresponding analyses aimed at understanding both the convergence of CoT length and the generalization of reasoning tasks, thereby addressing the key theoretical gaps previously identified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we provide a systematic analysis of the misalignment between the current token-level RL formulation and the reasoning-level CoT framework. Subsequently, we introduce our reasoning-level theoretical framework, CoT-Space. We then leverage this framework in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to analyze the convergence of CoT length in the reasoning process from the perspective of noise and risk, demonstrating its potential theoretical value. We perform empirical validation of our theoretical insights in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Following this, we briefly review related works in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Related Work ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and finally conclude the paper in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conclusion ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04027v1/internal-slow-thinking.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/263;" width="476" height="263" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An illustration of the analogy between strategy discovery in classical RL and RL for LLM reasoning.</span> (a) In a classical RL scenario such as a Brick Breaker game, the agent is given a high-level goal to break more bricks. Through exploration, it discovers a non-trivial, effective strategy to hit the ball behind the bricks to maximize its reward. (b) In LLM reasoning, the policy model is given a high-level goal to provide the correct answers. As RL training progresses, the model autonomously discovers that generating a suitable CoT is an effective strategy to achieve the goal. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a systematic analysis of the limitations inherent in the current token-level RL formulation when applied to the higher-order cognitive processes of CoT reasoning. We first discuss the fundamental misalignment of this conventional approach in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 From Token-Level to Reasoning-Level Perspective ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and then discuss the continuum nature of the reasoning-level state space in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Finally, we introduce our novel, reasoning-level theoretical framework CoT-Space in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>From Token-Level to Reasoning-Level Perspective</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conventional theoretical framework for applying RL to LLMs is fundamentally misaligned with the reasoning-level nature of CoT scenarios. This token-level assumption is inherited from traditional RL frameworks, where the agent receives a reliable and meaningful reward at each state transition. For instance, classic RL tasks like chess, Go, or video games allow for a meaningful measurement of a state’s value, which guides the agent’s policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, in the context of natural language, the auto-regressive nature of modern language models has led us to formulate each token prediction as a state transition, resulting in a popular token-level theoretical framework. We briefly review this formulation here. Prior works usually model natural language generation as a token-level Markov Decision Process (MDP): <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},r,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},r,H)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the state space of existing token sequences, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the action space representing the token dictionary, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function guiding generation, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the token budget.
A policy <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{S}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the next token, and the state transitions by concatenating the predicted token <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pi(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pi(s)</annotation></semantics></math> to the existing sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation presents a significant challenge regarding the state space <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as the following assertion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assertion">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assertion 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The token-level state space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of natural language exhibits both <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exponential growth</span> and <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">discrete topology</span>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the one hand, the state space <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into a union of infinite subspaces based on sequence length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{0}\cup\mathcal{S}_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\mathcal{S}_{t}\cup\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_{0}\cup\mathcal{S}_{1}\cup\cdots\cup\mathcal{S}_{t}\cup\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{t}=\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{t}=\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the combinations of dictionary tokens grow exponentially, the subspace for a given prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{|q|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{|q|}</annotation></semantics></math>, and all subsequent subspaces <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{&gt;|q|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{&gt;|q|}</annotation></semantics></math> are astronomically large. Consequently, the data observed during RL training constitutes only a minute fraction of the entire huge possible token-level state space.
On the other hand, the transition between states is unidirectional and discrete due to the auto-regressive nature of the process: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathcal{S}_{t},\mathcal{T}(\bm{s}_{t},\pi)\in\mathcal{S}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathcal{S}_{t},\mathcal{T}(\bm{s}_{t},\pi)\in\mathcal{S}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This indicates the discrete nature of the token-level state space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Turning to CoT reasoning processes, we observe that a complete reasoning trajectory of length <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> comprises multiple conceptual steps <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> with each step
<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> realized through multiple token generations. Consider solving algebraic equations: each <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a specific subgoal in the solution process, achieved through coordinated token generation. This perspective reveals two fundamentally distinct procedural layers:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning-Level:</span> The strategic planning of correct reasoning steps to solve problems</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-Level:</span> The tactical execution of individual reasoning steps through token generation</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional MDP formulations reduce LLM reasoning to token-level exploration, while the essence of reasoning involves higher-order cognitive processes that orchestrate sequences of conceptual steps. The token-level perspective fails to capture the strategic planning and structural coherence inherent in human-like reasoning, creating substantial theoretical barriers to understanding modern reasoning paradigms such as CoT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, to establish theoretically sound foundations, we must transition from token-level formulations to reasoning-level perspectives that properly reflect the problem’s intrinsic nature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon Assertion <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Assertion 2.1. ‣ 2.1 From Token-Level to Reasoning-Level Perspective ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, which characterizes the token-level state space as both high-dimensional and discrete, we now provide a theoretical basis for approximating this space as a continuum at the reasoning level. The core insight is that while the token-level space consists of discrete points, the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantic</span> space it represents becomes increasingly dense as the reasoning process unfolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The essence of reasoning lies in the semantic meaning of conceptual steps, not in the specific tokens used to express them. A single reasoning step, such as “calculating the total weekly pages” of the instance in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, can be realized by a vast number of distinct token sequences:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Step 2: He writes 6*2=12 pages every week.”</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Then, I’ll calculate the total weekly pages: 6 pages/friend * 2 friends = 12 pages.”</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“So, for both friends, the page count per week is 6 <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 2, which equals 12.”</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">These sequences correspond to distant, discrete states in the token-level space. However, in a higher-dimensional semantic space, they are nearly identical, occupying a very small neighborhood. The CoT-Space framework posits that as the CoT length <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the number of semantically meaningful states grows so rapidly that the space they inhabit can be treated as a continuous manifold. This density arises from the <span id="S2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expressive redundancy</span> of language: the number of valid token-level paths to achieve a reasoning goal grows exponentially with the available token budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this, we introduce the following definitions and a key assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Semantic equivalence set.) For an abstract reasoning step <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a calculation, a logical deduction), its semantic equivalence set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> is the collection of all token sequences <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\mathcal{A}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\mathcal{A}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that are semantically equivalent to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})=\{\tau\in\mathcal{A}^{*}\ |\ \text{Decode}(\tau)\equiv\xi_{l}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Decode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})=\{\tau\in\mathcal{A}^{*}\ |\ \text{Decode}(\tau)\equiv\xi_{l}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of all possible token sequences and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Decode}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Decode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Decode}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is an abstract function mapping a token sequence to its semantic meaning.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Number of realizations.) For a complete reasoning trajectory of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> steps, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},...,\xi_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},...,\xi_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, its total number of token-level realizations, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{realize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">realize</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{realize}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the size of the Cartesian product of the semantic equivalence sets for each step.</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{realize}}(\xi_{1},...,\xi_{L})=\prod_{l=1}^{L}|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">realize</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{realize}}(\xi_{1},...,\xi_{L})=\prod_{l=1}^{L}|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l})|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Reasoning state density.) Within a fixed semantic volume <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">semantic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of all valid, reachable reasoning states of reasoning token amount <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">reasoning</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The state density <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the number of such states per unit of semantic volume.</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(K)=\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">reasoning</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">semantic</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(K)=\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To substantiate our claim that this density grows exponentially, we propose the following intuitive assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Exponential expressive redundancy of language.) For any non-trivial reasoning step <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, the size of its semantic equivalence set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l},k)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l},k)|</annotation></semantics></math>, when realized using an average of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, grows exponentially with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, there exists a constant <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l},k)|\geq c^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}(\xi_{l},k)|\geq c^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is grounded in the combinatorial nature of language; allowing even a few extra tokens for modifiers, rephrasing, or synonyms creates an exponentially larger space of semantically equivalent expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these foundations, we can now formally state the theorem that justifies the continuum approximation of reasoning-level state space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Continuum convergence of reasoning-level state space.)
Under Assumption <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Assumption 2.5. ‣ 2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, the reasoning-level state density, denoted as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, increases exponentially with the total reasoning token amount <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This exponential growth implies that the expected semantic distance between any two adjacent valid reasoning states decreases dramatically as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases,
and approaches zero as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, in the macroscopic limit, the discrete reasoning-level state space converges to a continuous semantic manifold.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6. ‣ 2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> provides the formal justification for the central premise of our work. This result licenses our departure from the discrete, token-level MDP formulation and enables the adoption of more powerful, analytically tractable tools from continuous optimization theory. With the continuum nature of the reasoning space established, we are now positioned to formally introduce our framework. In the subsequent section, we will leverage this continuum perspective to define the CoT-Space and reframe the LLM reasoning process as an optimization problem within this newly characterized landscape.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Formulation of CoT-Space</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the discussion above, which characterizes the token-level state space and illustrates the continuum nature of reasoning-level state space, we now develop a reasoning-level theoretical framework designed to bridge the analytical gap. This alternative perspective draws an analogy to interstellar travel in space, as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. From a token-level perspective, each individual token and its corresponding state are like discrete points, or stars, in a vast semantic galaxy. The auto-regressive generation process then resembles a journey across these stars. Since transitions only occur between these discrete states, the generation process is fundamentally discrete at the token-level scale, as illustrated in panel (a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, from the reasoning-level perspective, a continuum approximation emerges when we “zoom out” to consider the limiting behavior of this discrete system. Due to the exponential density of semantically valid states, the global properties of the space converge toward continuum-like characteristics, as visualized in panel (b). This macroscopic viewpoint allows us to move beyond individual tokens and analyze reasoning from a more abstract, strategic level.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><img src="2509.04027v1/space-by-diff-scale.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/127;" width="238" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An illustration for the shift from a token-level to a reasoning-level perspective.</span> (a) The traditional token-level scale views the generation process as a sequence of discrete transitions in a vast state space. (b) The proposed reasoning-level scale zooms out to view the state space from a macroscopic perspective, where the incredibly dense and numerous token-level states can be approximated as a continuous semantic reasoning space.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To enable this higher-level analysis, we provide the following definitions for a new formalization of LLM reasoning via RL. We begin by defining the reasoning-level state. Based on the CoT setting, where the output is generated step-by-step and consists of multiple intermediate thoughts, a reasoning state is defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(CoT reasoning state.) Given a query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and an incomplete set of intermediate reasoning steps <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, the current reasoning state is defined as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}=(q,\xi_{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}=(q,\xi_{o})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7. ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> establishes a reasoning-level state that is a more meaningful unit of analysis than a single token. We then introduce a special class of states that represent successful problem solutions, which we call <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimums</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Minimums in reasoning space.) Given a query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with a definite golden answer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all reasonable intermediate reasoning process for query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the minimums of the reasoning space are defined as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{M}_{q}=\left\{(q,\bm{\xi},\phi)~|~\bm{\xi}\in\Xi_{q}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo rspace="0.052em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{M}_{q}=\left\{(q,\bm{\xi},\phi)~|~\bm{\xi}\in\Xi_{q}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">According to Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Definition 2.8. ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, minimums are special states where the query has been successfully solved. For any intermediate reasoning state, we can then define which minimums are reachable from it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Reachable minimums.) Given a query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with a definite golden answer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and an incomplete set of intermediate reasoning steps <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{o}=[\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{o}=[\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The reachable minimums are defined as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{R}^{o}_{q}=\left\{(q,\bm{\xi},\phi)~|~\xi_{o}\sqsubseteq\bm{\xi}~\text{and}~\bm{\xi}\in\Xi_{q}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo rspace="0.052em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>⊑</mo><mrow><mi>𝛏</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛏</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{R}^{o}_{q}=\left\{(q,\bm{\xi},\phi)~|~\xi_{o}\sqsubseteq\bm{\xi}~\text{and}~\bm{\xi}\in\Xi_{q}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\sqsubseteq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⊑</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sqsubseteq b</annotation></semantics></math> represents that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is prefix of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Definition 2.9. ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> established the concept of reachable minimums. This concept represents the set of successful reasoning paths that can be completed from the current state. Building on this, we quantify the distance between a state and its reachable minimums and define the nearest minimum.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Reachable distance and nearest reachable minimum.) Given a query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with a definite golden answer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reasoning state <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> on this query, the reachable distance between the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and a reachable minimum is defined as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(s_{o},m)=|\bm{\xi}|-|\xi_{o}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝛏</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(s_{o},m)=|\bm{\xi}|-|\xi_{o}|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\bm{R}^{o}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>o</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\bm{R}^{o}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> are intermediate steps of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. The nearest minimum is defined as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}_{o}=\underset{m\in\bm{R}^{o}_{q}}{\arg\min}\,\operatorname{dist}(s_{o},m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>o</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐑</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">o</mi></msubsup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}_{o}=\underset{m\in\bm{R}^{o}_{q}}{\arg\min}\,\operatorname{dist}(s_{o},m)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Definition 2.10. ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> quantifies the number of reasoning steps required to reach the closest successful solution from a given state. Finally, we define a reasoning loss to characterize the gap between the current state and the optimal solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Reasoning loss.) Given a query <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with a definite golden answer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reasoning state <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> on this query, there exists a measure <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that quantifies the loss of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> towards the golden answer, which satisfies: for two different states <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i},s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i},s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(s_{i})&lt;C(s_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s_{i})&lt;C(s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(s_{i},m^{*}_{i})&lt;\operatorname{dist}(s_{j},m^{*}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(s_{i},m^{*}_{i})&lt;\operatorname{dist}(s_{j},m^{*}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Specially, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall m\in\bm{M}_{q},C(m)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall m\in\bm{M}_{q},C(m)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Definition 2.11. ‣ 2.3 Formulation of CoT-Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> conceptualizes the reasoning process as a descent in a loss landscape. The function <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a proxy for the distance to a correct solution, where a lower loss indicates a more advanced stage of reasoning. For instance, consider the problem in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. A state <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the intermediate thought <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“He writes <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> pages every week”</span> is closer to the final answer than a state <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with only <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“He writes each friend <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32=6</annotation></semantics></math> pages a week.”</span> According to this definition, because <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> requires fewer steps to reach a minimum (i.e., <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(s_{i},m_{i}^{*})&lt;\operatorname{dist}(s_{j},m_{j}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(s_{i},m_{i}^{*})&lt;\operatorname{dist}(s_{j},m_{j}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>), its reasoning loss must be lower, satisfying <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(s_{i})&lt;C(s_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s_{i})&lt;C(s_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">With this complete reasoning-level perspective, we can now model the LLM reasoning process as an optimization problem rather than a token-level MDP. This formulation simplifies the analytical foundation by reframing the process as an update to the reasoning state, guiding it toward one of the solution minima.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section demonstrates the analytical utility of the CoT-Space framework by applying it to a critical issue in LLM reasoning: the existence of an optimal thought process length and the related problem of “overthinking”. Moving beyond traditional analyses, we utilize the continuous perspective of CoT-Space to provide a theoretical foundation for this empirical phenomenon. Our analysis proceeds in two parts: we first examine the reasoning process through the lens of optimization and noise (Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and second, we assess its generalization capabilities from the perspective of risk (Subsection <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). Finally, we briefly conclude our theoretical insights in Subsection <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Takeaways ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. This application of CoT-Space showcases its potential to ground empirical findings in a robust theoretical model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.04027v1/optimization.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/167;" width="476" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An illustration of LLM reasoning modeled as an optimization process.</span> (a) In traditional ML, the goal is parameter optimization, where an algorithm iteratively calculates a gradient from data to update model parameters and converge to a minimum in the loss landscape. (b) In the CoT-Space framework, LLM reasoning is framed as state optimization, where the policy takes the current state and instruction to generate a new reasoning step, thereby updating the state to move closer to a solution minimum. (c) This panel visualizes the optimization process on a reasoning loss landscape, analogizing the CoT length to the learning rate in ML. A suitable CoT length, much like an optimal learning rate, navigates the space effectively to converge to a high-quality, generalizable solution. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By treating reasoning as an optimization process, we can draw a direct comparison to traditional machine learning (ML), as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As shown in subgraph (a), traditional ML optimization algorithms, such as SGD or Adam, formulate a cycle where the algorithm calculates a gradient based on the current parameters and training data to iteratively update the model. Similarly, in subgraph (b), when an LLM performs reasoning, its internal policy takes the current reasoning state and query instruction as input. It then generates a new step, which updates the state by concatenating the new output. This process establishes a parallel optimization cycle for LLM reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such a comparison inspires an application of the sharpness-aware minimization theorem from classical ML. This theorem posits that an optimal amount of noise in the optimization process leads to a converged minimum with better generalization ability. As shown in subgraph (c) with the blue legends, the learning rate can introduce noise into the gradient descent process. A learning rate that is too large will fail to converge, while one that is too small may converge to a sharp, low-quality minimum. Only a suitable learning rate introduces the right amount of noise to converge to a flat, high-quality minimum.
Given the similarities in the nature of optimization, this theoretical analysis can be applied to the reasoning process of LLMs, as shown with the orange legends in subgraph (c). In this analogy, the CoT length is analogous to the learning rate. A CoT that is too short may fail to produce a correct answer, while a CoT that is too long may lead to “overthinking”, resulting in a lower-quality response. Therefore, a suitable CoT length is essential for generating a high-quality response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this, we can model the optimization of LLM reasoning as a form of reasoning loss descent and perform a theoretical analysis. As previously established, when viewed from a reasoning-level perspective, the reasoning space can be considered approximately continuous, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{o}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{o}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous space. The state is then optimized continuously based on the policy and reasoning loss. This perspective enables us to analyze the reasoning process through a loss-reduction lens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For an <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-step CoT reasoning process, this can be viewed as an <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-step optimization based on the gradient of the reasoning loss. A single step of this optimization can be written as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta s=-\frac{1}{L}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\hat{C}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta s=-\frac{1}{L}\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}\hat{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a noisy estimation of the reasoning loss, influenced by the policy and random sampling. We assume that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random variable with expectation <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total reasoning steps, or the CoT length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the above gradient descent formulation, this optimization process can also be modeled as a stochastic differential equation (SDE) with <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> steps:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{\mathrm{d}t}=-\frac{\mathrm{d}C}{\mathrm{d}s}+\eta(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}s}{\mathrm{d}t}=-\frac{\mathrm{d}C}{\mathrm{d}s}+\eta(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise term and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t={1,\dots,L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t={1,\dots,L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume the noise term <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is white noise with the statistical properties: <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\eta(t)\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\eta(t)\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math> and autocorrelation <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle=gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle=gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise scale, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)</annotation></semantics></math> s a function characterizing the magnitude of the reasoning loss estimation error at state <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Equations (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) formulate the optimization process of CoT reasoning from the gradient update and SDE perspective, respectively. By combining the two equations related to noise, we can thus obtain the relationship between noise scale and CoT length, as shown in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Relationship between noise scale and CoT length.) In a given optimization space, the noise scale <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is inversely proportional to the reasoning length <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\propto\frac{1}{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\propto\frac{1}{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> clarifies the relationship between the noise <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and the CoT length <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> in the same the reasoning process.
According to the sharpness-aware minimization theorems, given a reasoning task, there exists an optimal noise scale <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the reasoning process, which corresponds to an optimal CoT length <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}\propto\frac{1}{g_{\text{opt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}\propto\frac{1}{g_{\text{opt}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In essence, this theoretical analysis provides a fundamental basis for why an optimal CoT length is crucial for effective reasoning, highlighting its role in introducing the right amount of noise to achieve a flat, high-quality, and generalizable solution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous section considered reasoning from the perspective of noise and analyzed it through an optimization lens. Here, we analyze the generalization risk of a policy to provide further theoretical insights, focusing on the upper bounds of reasoning risks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of CoT-Space, risks can be defined similarly to classical ML. The total expected error of a policy <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on the true data distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]</annotation></semantics></math>, can be decomposed into two distinct components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)=\underbrace{\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)}_{\text{Empirical Loss (Bias)}}+\underbrace{\left(R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\right)}_{\text{Generalization Error (Variance)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Empirical Loss (Bias)</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Generalization Error (Variance)</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)=\underbrace{\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)}_{\text{Empirical Loss (Bias)}}+\underbrace{\left(R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\right)}_{\text{Generalization Error (Variance)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical loss on the training set. In the following parts, we will analyze how each component behaves as a function of the CoT complexity, which we proxy by the expected CoT length <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the reasoning generalization error as the difference between the true expected loss and the empirical loss. This error quantifies a policy’s ability to generalize its reasoning to unseen queries, a concept parallel to the generalization error in traditional machine learning theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Reasoning generalization error.) Given <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> queries <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled i.i.d. from a distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> learned from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, let’s denote <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{q}^{\pi}=\pi(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{q}^{\pi}=\pi(q)</annotation></semantics></math> as the end reasoning state output by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on query <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The reasoning generalization error of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To derive an upper bound for this error in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 3.2. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we can simply adapt the PAC-Bayes framework, which provides a powerful link between generalization, policy complexity, and data volume. For our analysis within CoT-Space, we establish the following components:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hypothesis Space <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> The space of all possible reasoning policies, denoted by <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our learned policy <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prior Distribution <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> A distribution over the policy space <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> reflecting our initial beliefs about which policies are "good," often favoring simpler ones.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Posterior Distribution <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> A distribution over <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> that is learned from the training data queries <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, concentrating on policies that perform well on that data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By applying the PAC-Bayes theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we obtain a bound on the expected true loss which, when rearranged, yields an upper bound on the expected generalization error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(PAC-Bayesian generalization upper bound for LLM reasoning.) With CoT-Space defined above, the policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on an i.i.d. drawn training set with size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,by assuming the reasoning loss is bounded in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, for any prior <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds for all posterior <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]\leq\sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)+\ln\frac{2n}{\delta}}{2n}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ln</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]\leq\sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)+\ln\frac{2n}{\delta}}{2n}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\ln\frac{Q(\pi)}{P(\pi)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ln</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\ln\frac{Q(\pi)}{P(\pi)}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the posterior and prior distributions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A detailed analysis is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Proof and Analysis of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> provides a powerful, high-probability upper bound on the expected reasoning generalization error for policies sampled from the learned posterior <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. While this PAC-Bayesian bound is a valid, generic result, we can derive a more specific bound by using information theory, a perspective that connects the generalization error to the mutual information between the learned policy and the training data. This approach leads to the following information-theoretic upper bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Information-theoretical generalization upper bound for LLM reasoning.) With CoT-Space defined above, if the policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on an i.i.d. drawn training set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the following upper bound for the reasoning generalization error holds:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{E}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]|\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{max}^{2}\cdot\left(\mathbb{E}_{\pi,S}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}_{\pi,S}[|\xi|]\right)\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|}{2n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{E}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]|\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{max}^{2}\cdot\left(\mathbb{E}_{\pi,S}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}_{\pi,S}[|\xi|]\right)\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|}{2n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum possible value of the reasoning loss <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of reasoning steps in a CoT data, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\xi_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\xi_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tokens in the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th reasoning step in a CoT data.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A brief proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> explicitly connects the generalization error to the observable, structural properties of the reasoning process. It suggests that overfitting, or “overthinking”, can occur in two distinct ways: through excessive reasoning depth (high <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L]</annotation></semantics></math>) or through excessive step verbosity (high <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]</annotation></semantics></math>), both resulting in overlong response length. This provides a direct theoretical basis for regularizing the CoT generation process in RL to improve reasoning generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical loss, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, measures a policy’s ability to solve the problems it has already encountered. For complex reasoning tasks, a certain minimum number of steps is required for a correct solution. Intuitively,
if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the policy lacks the necessary “thinking space” to solve the queries, even those in the training set. This leads to high empirical loss and underfitting.
As <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the policy has more capacity to find the correct reasoning path, so the empirical loss <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> generally decreases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize this, we quantify the notion of problem complexity and establish a condition for policy failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Required reasoning depth.)
For any given query <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we define its Required Reasoning Depth, denoted as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, as the minimum number of CoT steps required to reach any success state <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> from the initial state <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math>. This value is an intrinsic property of the query’s complexity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 3.5. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> can be likened to the minimum number of steps required to solve a reasoning problem. For instance, a simple two-digit addition problem might require only one or two mental steps, making its <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> very low. In contrast, proving a complex theorem, such as the Pythagorean theorem, requires a sequence of many interconnected logical steps, resulting in a high <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. A policy that attempts to solve the theorem with only a few steps, regardless of how smart it is, will inevitably fail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the concept of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we now formalize the direct consequence of a policy’s generated reasoning being too shallow. We make a straightforward assumption about the relationship between insufficient reasoning depth and policy failure, which translates a structural property of the CoT into a quantifiable error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Policy failure on insufficient depth)
When a policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> generates a CoT of length <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for a query <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, if the length is insufficient to solve the problem, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}&lt;L^{*}(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}&lt;L^{*}(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy is guaranteed to fail. The resulting reasoning loss <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore bounded below by a significant positive constant, which we denote as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{fail}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{fail}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In the worst case, this loss can be as high as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Assumption 3.6. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> claims that a policy’s failure due to insufficient depth can be observed in practice. Consider a complex physics problem requiring multiple steps: identifying the forces, setting up equations, and solving for variables. A policy that only completes the first step and then stops will not be able to reach the correct final answer. In this case, the loss function would assign a high penalty (our <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{fail}</annotation></semantics></math>) because the generated CoT, while possibly correct for the initial step, is fundamentally incomplete for solving the problem. The policy, therefore, cannot even fit the training data for problems that require deeper reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With this framework established by Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 3.5. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Assumption 3.6. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, we can derive a lower bound for the empirical reasoning loss <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With CoT-Space defined above, if the policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on an i.i.d. drawn training set, the following lower bound for the empirical reasoning risk holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})\cdot C_{fail}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})\cdot C_{fail},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})=n_{fail}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})=n_{fail}/n</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical failure rate of policy <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on the training set.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A detailed proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> provides a formal basis for the “underfitting” portion of our trade-off curve. It demonstrates that the empirical loss is directly constrained by the probability that the policy generates a CoT that is too short for the problem’s intrinsic complexity. A policy that tends to produce short CoTs (i.e., has a small <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L]</annotation></semantics></math>) will have a high failure rate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> on any dataset containing non-trivial problems. Consequently, its empirical loss <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is guaranteed to be high. This formalizes why a policy must learn to increase its CoT length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve a low empirical loss, thus completing our explanation for the tradeoff in our total error analysis and the existence of an optimal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Tradeoff between Overfitting and Underfitting Risk</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we provide an upper bound for the generalization error in the reasoning process with relation to CoT length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. While in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, we provide a lower bound for the empirical loss in the reasoning process with relation to CoT length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the total expected error <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of a decreasing function (empirical loss) and an increasing function (generalization error), this classic Bias-Variance Trade-off inevitably leads to a U-shaped curve for the total error as a function of the CoT length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
For small <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the total error is high due to high bias (underfitting). The policy can’t solve even the training problems.
For large <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the total error is high due to high variance (overfitting). The policy has too much capacity and learns non-generalizable details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, an optimal length, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, must exist that minimizes the total error. This <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is long enough to avoid underfitting but short enough to prevent significant overfitting. Our analysis reveals that there is no universal best CoT length, instead, the existence of an optimal length is a natural consequence of the fundamental trade-off between fitting the training data and generalizing to new data. From this perspective, any RL algorithm that can explore effectively can eventually converge to this optimal strategy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Takeaways</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have analyzed with the CoT-Space from two perspectives: that of noise (Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and risk (Subsection <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). Based on the theoretical insights from Theorems <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, we can draw the following key conclusions about the factors that govern the convergence of the optimal CoT length <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> in LLM reasoning via RL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1. </span>
The intrinsic difficulty of a reasoning task is a primary determinant of the optimal CoT length. According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, a more challenging task necessitates a greater <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve a low empirical loss and avoid underfitting. Consequently, the optimal CoT length, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, must be sufficiently large to accommodate the task’s complexity, otherwise the policy’s failure rate will remain high. This insight formalizes the intuitive idea that complex problems require more thinking steps to be solved correctly, and any effective policy must learn to generate a CoT of a length commensurate with the problem’s difficulty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2. </span>
The capacity of the policy model directly influences the optimal CoT length by affecting the risk of overfitting. A model with higher capacity (e.g., more parameters) can explore a more expansive reasoning state space. This expanded exploration enables it to access states with higher reasoning losses that are inaccessible to weaker models, leading to a larger effective <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>. The generalization bound in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is an increasing function of both <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> and the CoT length <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, to mitigate the heightened overfitting risk posed by its own high capacity and the resulting larger effective <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>, a more powerful model must learn to regularize its output. It achieves this by converging to a smaller optimal CoT length (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>), thereby maintaining a low generalization error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3. </span>
While the choice of RL algorithm is crucial for effective training, our analysis suggests it has a limited direct influence on the final, converged CoT length. The optimal CoT length (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is primarily a function of the policy’s capacity and the inherent complexity of the training data. The RL algorithm’s main role is to find a policy that converges to this optimal length. As long as the algorithm is effective in its optimization, the final <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a property of the learned policy and the data distribution, rather than a byproduct of the specific RL approach used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4. </span>
In a given problem space, the scale of noise in the optimization process is inversely correlated with the converged CoT length. According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, when a policy is exploring the same task space, a higher level of noise will lead to a smaller converged optimal CoT length (<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>). This implies that a noisier optimization environment tends to favor simpler, shorter reasoning paths, likely because longer CoTs are more susceptible to the disruptive effects of noise. This finding underscores the importance of a stable training environment for achieving complex and in-depth reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To substantiate these theoretical derivations and demonstrate their practical relevance, we will now proceed to a series of empirical investigations in the following section. These experiments are designed to systematically test the four central remarks, providing robust empirical evidence for our proposed framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a series of experiments designed to empirically validate the theoretical insights established in the preceding sections. After detailing the experimental setup in Subsection <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we systematically investigate the relationship between the optimal CoT length (<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and key factors, including task difficulty, model capacity, the choice of RL algorithm, and optimization noise scale in Subsections <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Task Difficulty and Converged Length ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> through <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Noise Scale and Converged Length ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span> The experiments are conducted on the MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> benchmarks. The MATH training set is manually subdivided into five difficulty levels (Level 1–5) according to the original “level” field, where Level 5 is substantially more challenging than Level 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base Model</span> A diverse collection of pre-trained LLMs with varying architectures, parameter counts, and versions serves as the reference model for the reinforcement-learning algorithms. Specifically, the models include</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Qwen2.5 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>: Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-3B, Qwen2.5-1.5B, and Qwen2.5-0.6B.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Qwen3 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>: Qwen3-8B-Base, Qwen3-4B-Base, Qwen3-1.7B-Base, and Qwen3-0.6B-Base.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Llama3 series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, and Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The maximum response length is set to 2,048 tokens for the Qwen2.5 and Llama3 models and to 8,196 tokens for the reasoning-intensive Qwen3 models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm and Hyperparameters</span> The evaluation covers the current mainstream reinforcement-learning algorithms for mathematical reasoning: GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, Reinforce++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and RLOO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. The reward function is rule-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
For PPO, the critic model is initialized from Qwen2.5-0.6B. The learning rate for critic training is <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, the k3 KL-regularization coefficient is 0.001, and the GAE parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are both set to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. For DAPO, no KL regularization is applied; the high and low clip ratios are <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.28</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.20</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{cache}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,024</annotation></semantics></math>.
Across all algorithms, each training sample is sampled eight times (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math>). The actor is optimized with AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> at a learning rate of 1e-6, the training batch size is <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>, and training proceeds in a purely on-policy manner for up to 50 steps. All experiments are run on 8 <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 96 GB NVIDIA H20 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04027v1/experiment.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/313;" width="476" height="313"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Validation of the Factors Governing Optimal CoT Length (<math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> (a) The relationship between task difficulty and <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating that more complex tasks require longer reasoning process. (b) The inverse relationship between model capacity and <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where more powerful models converge to shorter CoTs to mitigate overfitting. (c) The convergence of mean response length under different RL algorithms, showing that the final <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is largely algorithm-agnostic. (d) The inverse correlation between optimization noise scale (proxied by KL-Loss) and <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicates that noisier training environments favor more concise reasoning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Task Difficulty and Converged Length</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To empirically validate Remark 1, we investigate the relationship between task difficulty and the converged optimal CoT length (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>). In this experiment, we utilize the converged mean response length of the policy (Qwen2.5-7B-Base) as a direct empirical measure for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The intrinsic difficulty of the task is stratified by using datasets of increasing complexity, namely GSM8K and the five manually curated levels of the MATH dataset (Level 1 to Level 5). As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a), there is a clear and monotonic trend: as the complexity of the task increases, the corresponding <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> also increases significantly. This result provides strong evidence for our theoretical claim. According to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, more challenging tasks possess a greater required reasoning depth (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>). Consequently, the policy must generate a longer CoT to avoid the high empirical loss associated with underfitting. The experimental outcome confirms that the RL process successfully identifies and converges to a longer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to accommodate the inherent complexity of more difficult problems. We also provide validations on more base models in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B More Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Model Capacity and Converged Length</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection examines the influence of model capacity on the optimal CoT length, thereby verifying Remark 2. We train the models by GRPO, then use the parameter count of different models as a proxy for their capacity and the final critic score as a measure of their reasoning performance. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b) plots the converged CoT length (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) against this critic score for various models trained on MATH. The results reveal a distinct negative correlation: within each model family (e.g., Llama 3, Qwen 2.5, and Qwen 3), larger and higher-performing models consistently converge to a shorter <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This empirical finding aligns precisely with our theoretical analysis. As stated in Remark 2 and derived from the generalization bound in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, models with higher capacity are inherently more susceptible to overfitting. The risk of overfitting is amplified by longer and more complex CoTs. Therefore, to minimize the total reasoning error, a more powerful model learns to regularize its reasoning process by adopting a more concise, shorter CoT, effectively balancing the trade-off between empirical performance and generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>RL Algorithm and Converged Length</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we assess the impact of different RL algorithms on the final converged CoT length to test the validity of Remark 3. We track the mean response length over the course of training for five different RL algorithms: REINFORCE++, RLOO, PPO, DAPO, and GRPO. The resulting dynamics are displayed in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c). While the convergence trajectories and learning speeds differ markedly among the algorithms—for example, GRPO gradually increases the response length, whereas RLOO maintains a high length from the outset—all tested algorithms ultimately steer the policy toward a similar range of converged mean response lengths for a given task and model. This outcome supports our insight in Remark 3 that the choice of RL algorithm is primarily a facilitator of the optimization process rather than a determinant of the optimal solution’s properties. The final converged length, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, appears to be an intrinsic characteristic dictated by the interplay between model capacity and task difficulty, not an artifact of the specific optimization algorithm used to find it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Noise Scale and Converged Length</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we validate Remark 4 by analyzing the relationship between the converged CoT length and the noise scale of the optimization process. For this analysis, we employ the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence loss, or “KL-Loss”, as a quantifiable proxy for optimization noise. A higher KL-Loss indicates a greater deviation from the reference policy, representing a less stable and thus “noisier” update. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(d) illustrates the relationship between the final KL-Loss and the converged length (<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) across various models and task difficulties. The data shows a clear inverse correlation: for a given task, models that exhibit a higher KL-Loss consistently converge to a shorter <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This empirical result substantiates the relationship derived in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. A noisier optimization environment penalizes longer, more intricate reasoning paths, as they are more susceptible to disruption from unstable policy updates. Consequently, the RL process naturally favors shorter and more robust CoTs to ensure stable convergence, confirming that a higher noise scale leads to a smaller optimal CoT length.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Reasoning with LLMs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have significantly enhanced their capacity for complex reasoning. Building upon the foundational concept of LLMs as few-shot learners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, a pivotal development has been the use of prompting techniques to elicit explicit reasoning processes. Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, for instance, guides models to generate intermediate steps, a method whose robustness was later improved by Self-Consistency through the aggregation of multiple reasoning paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Theoretical understanding has advanced in parallel, with information-theoretic frameworks offering methods to quantify and mitigate reasoning errors, thus bridging empirical and theoretical work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Concurrently, explorations into the representation of reasoning have yielded novel formats, from structured code in Program-of-Thought <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> to internal thought tokens generated within the model’s latent space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. However, these explorations have been largely empirical, often lacking a strong theoretical foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>RL for LLM Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Test-time scaling has emerged as an effective technique for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, with its underlying inference scaling laws having been empirically validated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Reinforcement Learning (RL) represents a prominent methodology for implementing these scaling techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. The application of RL to LLMs has spurred two parallel lines of inquiry. The first focuses on the development of novel frameworks for post-training, such as VC-PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and VAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The second line of inquiry seeks to elucidate the fundamental impact of RL on LLM behavior, where researchers have explored diverse questions. For instance, Setlur et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> demonstrated the conditional advantages of RL over Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). Yue et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> empirically investigated whether RL can expand the knowledge boundaries of LLMs, while Shao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> examined the influence of reward modeling. Concurrently, Zhao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> posited that RL primarily serves to amplify behaviors acquired during pre-training. Although these studies provide valuable insights from different perspectives, the absence of a common theoretical formulation can lead to divergent conclusions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Optimization and Generalization</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Optimization in machine learning refers to the process of finding the minima of a loss function during model training. A prominent framework in this domain is Sharpness-Aware Minimization (SAM), which operates on the principle that flatter minima correlate with better generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. This phenomenon was later attributed to the inherent noise within the optimization process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Building on this insight, subsequent works have proposed various techniques to enhance optimization, such as batch augmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, extrapolation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, and the introduction of a surrogate gap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
Generalization analysis evaluates a model’s ability to perform effectively on unseen data. Information theory provides a powerful theoretical foundation for this analysis. Key concepts include entropy, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, which measures the information content of a random variable <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and mutual information, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies the information shared between two variables. Information-theoretic approaches have found wide-ranging applications in machine learning, from establishing bounds on the generalization capacity of deep learning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> to enhancing model interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. More recently, these tools have been employed to analyze synthetic data generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and to quantify reasoning errors in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we addressed the critical misalignment between the token-level formulation of traditional reinforcement learning theories and the reasoning-level execution of Chain-of-Thought in Large Language Models. We introduced CoT-Space, a new theoretical framework that shifts the perspective from a discrete Markov Decision Process to a continuous optimization problem in a semantic reasoning space. This novel viewpoint models the generation of a reasoning chain as a trajectory towards a solution minimum in a reasoning loss landscape, providing a more intuitive and powerful lens for theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis, conducted within the CoT-Space, validates the effectiveness of this framework. From an optimization perspective, we established an analogy between CoT length and the learning rate in classical machine learning, demonstrating that an optimal reasoning length corresponds to an optimal noise scale that promotes convergence to flatter, more generalizable solutions. Furthermore, from a risk analysis perspective, we decomposed the total reasoning error into empirical loss and generalization error. We proved that there exists a fundamental trade-off: shorter CoTs risk underfitting due to insufficient reasoning depth, while longer CoTs risk overfitting by increasing model complexity. This trade-off theoretically grounding the existence of an optimal CoT length <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{opt}</annotation></semantics></math>, that minimizes total error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking forward, the CoT-Space framework opens up several promising avenues for future research. First, it can be extended to analyze the impact of different RL algorithms and reward structures on the reasoning process, potentially leading to more principled algorithm design. Second, the insights from our risk analysis could inspire new regularization techniques aimed at explicitly controlling CoT complexity to prevent overfitting and improve generalization. Finally, this framework could be applied to other forms of complex reasoning beyond CoT, such as tree-based or graph-based thought processes, thereby advancing our theoretical understanding and practical ability to build more capable and reliable AI reasoning systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
