<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proofs</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6. ‣ 2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, consider a single, fixed reasoning-level path <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\xi_{1},...,\xi_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\xi_{1},...,\xi_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each step <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is realized by an average of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. By Assumption <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Assumption 2.5. ‣ 2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, the number of token-level realizations for this single path, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{realize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>realize</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{realize}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is bounded below:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{realize}}\geq\prod_{l=1}^{L}c^{k}=(c^{k})^{L}=c^{kL}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>realize</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{realize}}\geq\prod_{l=1}^{L}c^{k}=(c^{k})^{L}=c^{kL}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that the number of token-level trajectories corresponding to even a single reasoning path grows exponentially with the total number of tokens, denoted as <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=kL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=kL</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we consider the total number of distinct, valid reasoning states, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>reasoning</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|</annotation></semantics></math>. For complex problems, the number of distinct valid reasoning paths itself grows with <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. As each additional reasoning step can potentially branch from multiple previous states, the number of reachable states grows super-linearly, often exponentially, with <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|\propto\alpha^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>reasoning</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|\propto\alpha^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> for some base <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 2.4. ‣ 2.2 On the Continuum Nature of the Reasoning-Level State Space ‣ 2 CoT-Space: A Reasoning-Level Theoretical Framework ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, the reasoning state density <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is thus:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(L)=\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}\propto\frac{\alpha^{K}}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>reasoning</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>semantic</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>semantic</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(L)=\frac{|\mathcal{S}_{\text{reasoning}}^{(K)}|}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}\propto\frac{\alpha^{K}}{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Assuming the semantic volume <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>semantic</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{semantic}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given problem domain is fixed, the state density <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(K)</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially with <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we connect high density to the continuum property. In a <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space, the expected distance <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]</annotation></semantics></math> between adjacent points <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i},s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i},s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is inversely related to the density, approximately scaling as <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]\propto\rho(K)^{-1/D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]\propto\rho(K)^{-1/D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(K)</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially, it follows that:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{K\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{K\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[d(s_{i},s_{j})]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">When the expected distance between states approaches zero, it implies that for any state and any direction, another valid state can be found within an infinitesimally small neighborhood. This is the defining characteristic of a continuous space or manifold. Therefore, as the reasoning token amount <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the discrete CoT-Space increasingly resembles a continuum, which validates the application of continuous optimization tools for its analysis.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">To bridge the discrete reasoning update with the continuous SDE model, we equate the variance induced by the noise term in both frameworks over a single reasoning step.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we analyze the noise in the discrete one-step reasoning update from Equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</span></p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta s=-\frac{1}{L}\left(\frac{dC}{ds}+\left(\frac{d\hat{C}}{ds}-\frac{dC}{ds}\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta s=-\frac{1}{L}\left(\frac{dC}{ds}+\left(\frac{d\hat{C}}{ds}-\frac{dC}{ds}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The noise originates from the estimation error, which we define as <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\left(\frac{d\hat{C}}{ds}-\frac{dC}{ds}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\left(\frac{d\hat{C}}{ds}-\frac{dC}{ds}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume this error has a variance <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\alpha^{2}\rangle=F(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\alpha^{2}\rangle=F(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is a function characterizing the magnitude of the reasoning loss estimation error at state <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The variance of the full noise term in the discrete update is therefore:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\left(-\frac{1}{L}\alpha\right)^{2}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{L^{2}}\langle\alpha^{2}\rangle=\frac{F(s)}{L^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\left(-\frac{1}{L}\alpha\right)^{2}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{L^{2}}\langle\alpha^{2}\rangle=\frac{F(s)}{L^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we analyze the noise from the SDE model proposed in Equation (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Reasoning as Optimization: A Perspective of Noise ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We assume the noise term <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is white noise with the following statistical properties: <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\eta(t)\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\eta(t)\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math> and autocorrelation <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle=gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle=gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise scale. To find the variance over a discrete step, we integrate the SDE over a time interval <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t=1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t=1/L</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta s=\int_{0}^{1/L}\left(-\frac{dC}{ds}+\eta(t)\right)dt=-\frac{1}{L}\frac{dC}{ds}+\int_{0}^{1/L}\eta(t)dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta s=\int_{0}^{1/L}\left(-\frac{dC}{ds}+\eta(t)\right)dt=-\frac{1}{L}\frac{dC}{ds}+\int_{0}^{1/L}\eta(t)dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The variance of the noise component of this update is:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\left(\int_{0}^{1/L}\eta(t)dt\right)^{2}\right\rangle=\int_{0}^{1/L}\int_{0}^{1/L}\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle dt^{\prime}dt=\int_{0}^{1/L}\int_{0}^{1/L}gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})dt^{\prime}dt=\frac{gF(s)}{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\left(\int_{0}^{1/L}\eta(t)dt\right)^{2}\right\rangle=\int_{0}^{1/L}\int_{0}^{1/L}\langle\eta(t)\eta(t^{\prime})\rangle dt^{\prime}dt=\int_{0}^{1/L}\int_{0}^{1/L}gF(s)\delta(t-t^{\prime})dt^{\prime}dt=\frac{gF(s)}{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By equating the variance from the discrete update (Eq. <a href="#A1.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Proof. ‣ A.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and the SDE model (Eq. <a href="#A1.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Proof. ‣ A.2 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we can solve for the noise scale <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{F(s)}{L^{2}}=\frac{gF(s)}{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{F(s)}{L^{2}}=\frac{gF(s)}{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Solving for <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we find:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\frac{1}{L}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\frac{1}{L}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">This result shows that the noise scale <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in our CoT-Space SDE model is inversely proportional to the CoT length <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Proof and Analysis of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s denote the true reasoning loss of a policy <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}}[C(s_{q}^{\pi})]</annotation></semantics></math> and the empirical reasoning loss as <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math>. The reasoning generalization error for a specific policy <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. We aim to bound this gap.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The PAC-Bayes theorem provides a bound on the expected true loss for policies drawn from the posterior <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. A standard version of the theorem is often presented as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(PAC-Bayes generalization upper bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.) For any loss function bounded in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, for any prior <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> over the draw of the training set <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds for all posterior distributions <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[R(\pi)]\leq\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]+\sqrt{\frac{KL(Q||P)+\ln\frac{2n}{\delta}}{2n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ln</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[R(\pi)]\leq\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]+\sqrt{\frac{KL(Q||P)+\ln\frac{2n}{\delta}}{2n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\ln\frac{Q(\pi)}{P(\pi)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ln</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi\sim Q}[\ln\frac{Q(\pi)}{P(\pi)}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between the posterior and prior distributions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s adapt Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1. ‣ Proof. ‣ A.3 Proof and Analysis of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> to our framework. We first need to normalize our reasoning loss <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to be in <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Assuming there is a maximum possible loss <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can define a normalized loss <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}(\cdot)=C(\cdot)/C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}(\cdot)=C(\cdot)/C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math>. The bound then applies to the normalized losses <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}^{\prime}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}^{\prime}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity of notation, let’s assume <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is already normalized in <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Information-Theoretic Interpretation</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bound has a clear information-theoretic meaning, which aligns with the goal of understanding reasoning phenomena:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="A1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity Term (<math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The KL divergence term measures the information gain in moving from our prior belief <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to our posterior belief <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> after seeing the data. It quantifies the complexity of the learned policy distribution.</p>
<ul id="A1.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is very different from <math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)</annotation></semantics></math> is large), it means we had to learn a great deal from the training data to find a good policy. This is analogous to overfitting or memorizing the training set. The bound becomes looser (larger), penalizing this complexity and suggesting a higher risk of poor generalization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> remains close to <math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A1.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)</annotation></semantics></math> is small), it implies the learned policy did not stray far from our initial bias towards simpler policies. The bound is tighter, suggesting better generalization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Term (<math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The bound depends on the number of training queries <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the term under the square root decreases, making the bound tighter. This confirms the intuition that having more data leads to better generalization and reduces the gap between training and test performance.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, this PAC-Bayes bound formalizes the trade-off in learning a reasoning policy. To achieve good generalization, a policy <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> must not only achieve a low empirical reasoning loss <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> on the queries it has seen, but it must also remain "simple" in an information-theoretic sense, by not deviating too much from a reasonable prior. This framework provides a principled way to analyze and potentially control for phenomena like "overthinking" by viewing it as a form of overfitting, which would correspond to a large <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(Q||P)</annotation></semantics></math> term.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2.1 An Upper Bound for Generalization Error: The Overfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The core idea is that a policy can only overfit to the training set <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{q_{1},\dots,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{q_{1},\dots,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> by encoding information about <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> within its own parameters. The mutual information <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)</annotation></semantics></math> precisely quantifies this amount of encoded information. A key result from learning theory provides the following bound on the expected generalization error:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Information-theoretical generalization upper bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.) With CoT-Space defined above, if the policy <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on an i.i.d. drawn training set <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with size <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the following upper bound for the reasoning generalization error holds:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{E}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]|\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{max}^{2}\cdot I(\pi;S)}{2n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{E}[R(\pi)-\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)]|\leq\sqrt{\frac{C_{max}^{2}\cdot I(\pi;S)}{2n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum possible value of the reasoning loss <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the expectation is over the random draw of the training set <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and the (potentially stochastic) output <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of the learning algorithm.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma A.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> transforms the problem of bounding generalization error into the problem of bounding the mutual information <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)</annotation></semantics></math>. To make this bound meaningful for our framework, we must connect <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)</annotation></semantics></math> to the structural properties of the generated Chain-of-Thought.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We then turn to bound mutual information by CoT complexity.
The policy <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> expresses the information it has learned from <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by generating a CoT, which is a sequence of reasoning steps <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=(\xi_{1},\xi_{2},\dots,\xi_{L})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each step <math id="A1.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is, in turn, a sequence of tokens. The total information capacity of the policy’s output is therefore determined not just by the number of steps, but by the total number of tokens generated.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us define:</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>: The number of reasoning steps in a CoT.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\xi_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\xi_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>: The number of tokens in the <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th reasoning step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{L}|\xi_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{L}|\xi_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>: The total number of tokens in the CoT.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The mutual information <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)</annotation></semantics></math> is upper-bounded by the entropy of the policy’s output, which represents its description length or information capacity. The total number of token-level decisions the policy makes determines this capacity. Let <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L]</annotation></semantics></math> be the expected number of reasoning steps (reasoning depth) and <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]</annotation></semantics></math> be the expected number of tokens per step (step verbosity). The expected total number of tokens is <math id="A1.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If we assume the policy generates tokens from a dictionary <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the information required to specify one token is at most <math id="A1.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log|\mathcal{A}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log|\mathcal{A}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we can establish the following upper bound on the mutual information.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In LLM reasoning scenarios, if the policy <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on a training set <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the mutual information <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\pi;S)\leq\mathbb{E}_{S,\pi}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{L}|\xi_{i}|\right]\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|=\left(\mathbb{E}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]\right)\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\pi;S)\leq\mathbb{E}_{S,\pi}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{L}|\xi_{i}|\right]\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|=\left(\mathbb{E}[L]\cdot\mathbb{E}[|\xi|]\right)\cdot\log|\mathcal{A}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma A.3. ‣ Proof. ‣ A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> formalizes the intuition that the information a policy learns from data cannot exceed the information content it is capable of expressing in its output. Together with Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma A.2. ‣ Proof. ‣ A.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, this finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2.2 A Lower Bound for Empirical Loss: The Underfitting Risk ‣ 3.2 Reasoning and Generalization: A Perspective of Risk ‣ 3 Analytical Applications for Overthinking within CoT-Space ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin with its definition:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">We can partition the sum over the training set into two disjoint subsets based on whether the generated CoT length was sufficient:</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set of failed queries: <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{fail}=\{q_{i}\in S\mid L_{i}&lt;L^{*}(q_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{fail}=\{q_{i}\in S\mid L_{i}&lt;L^{*}(q_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set of potentially successful queries: <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{succ}=\{q_{i}\in S\mid L_{i}\geq L^{*}(q_{i})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{succ}=\{q_{i}\in S\mid L_{i}\geq L^{*}(q_{i})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{fail}=|S_{fail}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{fail}=|S_{fail}|</annotation></semantics></math>. We can now rewrite the empirical loss as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{fail}}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})+\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{succ}}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)=\frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{fail}}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})+\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{succ}}C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">Applying our failure assumption, we know that for every <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in S_{fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in S_{fail}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})\geq C_{fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(s_{q_{i}}^{\pi})\geq C_{fail}</annotation></semantics></math>. For queries in <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{succ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{succ}</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss is at least <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows us to establish a lower bound:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq\frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{fail}}C_{fail}+\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{succ}}0\right)=\frac{n_{fail}\cdot C_{fail}}{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq\frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{fail}}C_{fail}+\sum_{q_{i}\in S_{succ}}0\right)=\frac{n_{fail}\cdot C_{fail}}{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text">We can define the empirical failure rate of the policy <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> on the training set as <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})=n_{fail}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})=n_{fail}/n</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to our final lower bound for the empirical loss:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})\cdot C_{fail}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{n}(\pi)\geq P_{\pi}(L&lt;L^{*})\cdot C_{fail}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text">This finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Experiments</h2>

<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.04027v1/experiment-supp.svg" id="A2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/109;" width="476" height="109"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A2.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supplementary validation of the relationship between task difficulty and optimal CoT length (<math id="A2.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) on additional models.</span> The plots illustrate that the converged mean response length (<math id="A2.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) consistently increases with task difficulty across three different models: (a) Qwen2.5-3B-Base, (b) Qwen2.5-1.5B-Base, and (c) Qwen2.5-0.5B-Base. These results reinforce the findings presented in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a) and demonstrate the robustness of Remark 1 across models of varying capacities.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further strengthen the empirical validation of Remark 1, we conducted supplementary experiments to verify the relationship between task difficulty and the optimal CoT length (<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) across models of varying capacities. The experimental protocol is identical to that described in Subsection <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Task Difficulty and Converged Length ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, but here we replicate the analysis on three additional models from the Qwen2.5 series: Qwen2.5-3B-Base, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Base, and Qwen2.5-0.5B-Base.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, presented in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B More Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, consistently corroborate the findings from our main experiment (Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ CoT-Space: A Theoretical Framework for Internal Slow-Thinking via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a)). Across all three smaller models, we observe the same clear, monotonic trend: the converged optimal CoT length (<math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) increases systematically as the difficulty of the task escalates from GSM8K to the higher levels of the MATH dataset. This demonstrates that the principle outlined in Remark 1, that more complex problems necessitate a longer reasoning process to avoid underfitting is not specific to a single high-capacity model but holds true as a general principle across models of different scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
